::Q1::What is the primary advantage of a NumPy array over a Python list for large numerical datasets?
{
~A NumPy array can store unlimited Python object types in the same array
=NumPy uses a fixed data type and contiguous memory, allowing operations to be performed efficiently
~NumPy automatically converts every numerical operation into a SQL query
~A NumPy array does not require memory to store its values
}

::Q2::A two-dimensional NumPy array is created with two rows, each containing three elements\: [1, 2, 3] and [4, 5, 6]. What are its shape and number of dimensions?
{
~shape (6,) and ndim 1
=shape (2, 3) and ndim 2
~shape (3, 2) and ndim 2
~shape (2, 3) and ndim 3
}

::Q3::A one-dimensional NumPy array contains the values 12, 45, 7, 88, and 23. It is filtered using a boolean condition that selects only values greater than 20. What does this filtering expression return?
{
~[12, 7]
=[45, 88, 23]
~[True, True, False, True, True]
~20
}

::Q4::A two-dimensional NumPy array has two rows\: [1, 2, 3] and [4, 5, 6]. A one-dimensional array [10, 20, 30] is added to it using NumPy's broadcasting rules. What is the result?
{
~[[11, 12, 13], [14, 15, 16]]
=[[11, 22, 33], [14, 25, 36]]
~[[10, 20, 30], [40, 50, 60]]
~An error because the arrays have different shapes
}

::Q5::A two-dimensional NumPy array has two rows\: [10, 20, 30] and [40, 50, 60]. When the sum is computed along axis 0 (summing down each column), what is the result?
{
~[60, 150]
=[50, 70, 90]
~210
~[10, 20, 30]
}

::Q6::Which statement correctly describes the difference between NumPy's arange function used with a start, stop, and step value, and its linspace function used with a start, stop, and a count of values?
{
~Both functions use a step size and exclude the endpoint
=arange uses a step size and excludes the endpoint, while linspace uses a number of values and includes the endpoint by default
~arange uses a number of values while linspace uses a step size
~Both functions always produce the same values
}